Result of calling the Read tool:
1	---
2	name: sign-in-is-the-in-app-form-everywhere
3	description: JusPrin account sign-in is the in-app form (Google button, emailed code, password) on every screen; the server's web sign-in page was an unapproved agent mistake
4	metadata:
5	  type: feedback
6	---
7	
8	Every place JusPrin signs someone in uses the in-app sign-in form that first launch has: "Continue with Google", an email field with a 6-digit code typed into the app, and "Use a password instead". That covers first launch, Home's AI group, Preferences › Account and the v1 welcome-back window. Kenneth decided this on 2026-11-07.
9	
10	Sending people to the server's own web sign-in page (`CloudBrowserStart::SignInPage`, `/o/authorize/` with no provider, used by `AgentHost::handle_account_sign_in`) was a mistake an earlier coding agent made without asking him. Code comments, tests or canvas boards that describe "the browser sign-in, whose page also signs up" or "the server's sign-in page offers email and Google both" come from that mistake. They are not a decision, so don't copy them or build on them.
11	
12	**Why:** Kenneth never approved the server-page path. It also means someone who signed up with an email code at first launch meets a different set of choices when signing in again from Preferences.
13	
14	Kenneth restated it later the same day: "using server page to sign in was A mistake earlier coding agents made without my asking me for approval ... we use the in-[app] form to sign in."
15	
16	**How to apply:** for any new sign-in entry point, reuse the first-launch form. On branch `ui-walkthrough` (uncommitted as of 2026-11-07) it is shared as `AgentUI/src/components/SignInDoor.tsx`, used by HomeUI `Onboarding.tsx`, Preferences and the welcome-back window. Before that change it lived only in HomeUI `Onboarding.tsx`. Grep for it before trusting either location. It is backed by `ICloudSignIn::start_email` / `send_code` / `submit_code` / `submit_password` and `CloudBrowserStart::Google`). The only browser step is Google's own page. If something seems to need a web page, ask Kenneth first. Related: [[first-run-flow-sequence]], [[first-run-flow-boards]].
17	

